From: portare-ch <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan/khr_display: a swap interval above 1 is a timed present, not a repeated one

The Vulkan driver keeps a swap interval above 1 by presenting each frame
interval times into a FIFO swapchain. On the KHR display context with
two swapchain images that leaves the core one refresh period: the second
present's acquire returns at the vblank between the two, and the core
has until the next one, 8.36 ms on a 120 Hz panel, or it costs a whole
refresh. A PlayStation core at 4x with a CRT shader misses often enough
to run at 55.65 fps against 59.83 and put gaps in the sound.

Mesa's KMS backend flips on the vblank a VK_GOOGLE_display_timing
desired present time names. So on that context each frame is presented
once, timed to reach the display interval refresh periods after the last
present the driver has timed, counted per present issued since it. The
acquire then returns when the previous frame reaches the display, and
the core has the whole frame period. The repeat loop stays for contexts
that cannot time a present.

Two things in the base code kept the extension's entry points from
being usable here at all: they were loaded under the Win32 ifdef only,
and a device the core negotiates (first interface: the frontend names
the required extensions and the core enables them; wrapper: the
frontend adds its optional ones itself) never had the timing extension
recorded as enabled. Both paths now carry the optional extensions, and
the entry points load on every platform. A core may still ignore the
list it is given: Flycast did, so its device lacked the extension and
its entry points resolved to NULL. The frontend then counts the
extension as absent, and presents without timing structs, rather than
trusting the list it passed.

Measured on the Retroid Pocket Nova, Tekken 3, threaded video off:
59.64 fps against 59.83 with 10 dropped frames in 2565, no gaps in a
15 s capture of the audio output, against 55.65 fps and 5 to 8 ms gaps
7.5% of the time before. The log says when it is in effect:
"Timed presents: swap interval 2, one present a frame, 16.683 ms apart."

Nothing here is specific to one device.
---
